1
00:00:01,164 --> 00:00:04,542
People worry about AI for all kinds of reasons.

2
00:00:04,542 --> 00:00:07,336
It's polarizing, it spreads misinformation,

3
00:00:07,336 --> 00:00:09,213
it's coming for our jobs.

4
00:00:09,213 --> 00:00:11,799
And those are all good reasons to be nervous.

5
00:00:11,799 --> 00:00:14,677
But what really keeps me up at night is something else.

6
00:00:14,677 --> 00:00:16,971
Something that feels more personal

7
00:00:16,971 --> 00:00:21,017
and perhaps even more concerning for the human experience itself.

8
00:00:21,017 --> 00:00:24,353
I worry that AI will make us boring.

9
00:00:24,353 --> 00:00:27,148
You, me, all of us.

10
00:00:27,148 --> 00:00:30,318
That's because the more we outsource our decisions to AI,

11
00:00:30,318 --> 00:00:33,362
the more we surrender something deeply human.

12
00:00:33,362 --> 00:00:35,615
Our capacity to explore,

13
00:00:35,615 --> 00:00:38,242
to take risks, to stumble into the unknown,

14
00:00:38,242 --> 00:00:41,204
and sometimes surprise even ourselves.

15
00:00:41,204 --> 00:00:45,333
I worry that without the potential for discovery, for serendipity,

16
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:49,712
we risk becoming more shallow and unidimensional versions of who we are

17
00:00:49,712 --> 00:00:51,339
and who we could be,

18
00:00:51,339 --> 00:00:56,177
not just as humans, as individuals, but as humanity.

19
00:00:56,177 --> 00:00:59,013
Now let me make this idea a bit more concrete

20
00:00:59,013 --> 00:01:03,976
and take you somewhere perhaps unexpected, but also totally relatable.

21
00:01:03,976 --> 00:01:06,521
Imagine walking into a Baskin-Robbins.

22
00:01:06,521 --> 00:01:08,606
What you'll see is an illustrious assortment

23
00:01:08,606 --> 00:01:10,566
of 31 different ice cream flavors.

24
00:01:10,566 --> 00:01:15,530
There's chocolate chip, pistachio, almond, lemon sorbet, and many more.

25
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:17,824
You're now facing a difficult choice.

26
00:01:17,824 --> 00:01:20,743
Are you going to go with an old favorite, chocolate maybe,

27
00:01:20,743 --> 00:01:23,579
or are you going to take a risk on something funky and new,

28
00:01:23,579 --> 00:01:27,041
like wild and reckless sherbet?

29
00:01:27,041 --> 00:01:28,626
And yes, it's a real flavor.

30
00:01:28,626 --> 00:01:30,294
(Laughter)

31
00:01:30,294 --> 00:01:32,922
The choice you're facing is a classic human dilemma

32
00:01:32,922 --> 00:01:37,343
scientists call the exploitation- exploration trade-off.

33
00:01:37,343 --> 00:01:41,013
Are you going to play it safe and capitalize on what you know you like,

34
00:01:41,013 --> 00:01:42,515
or are you going to take a risk

35
00:01:42,515 --> 00:01:45,476
with the hope of finding something even better?

36
00:01:45,476 --> 00:01:47,603
And our lives are full of these trade offs.

37
00:01:47,603 --> 00:01:51,232
Stick with your favorite restaurant or try the fancy new spot on the corner.

38
00:01:51,232 --> 00:01:54,443
Keep your usual haircut or ask for something new and edgy.

39
00:01:54,443 --> 00:01:55,945
Stay in your stable job

40
00:01:55,945 --> 00:01:59,115
or finally try to become a pickleball champion?

41
00:01:59,115 --> 00:02:01,909
(Laughter)

42
00:02:01,909 --> 00:02:06,581
We all differ in our appetite for exploitation versus exploration.

43
00:02:06,581 --> 00:02:08,916
But we're also all hardwired by evolution

44
00:02:08,916 --> 00:02:11,502
to strike a certain balance between the two,

45
00:02:11,502 --> 00:02:13,963
because for our ancestors on the African savanna,

46
00:02:13,963 --> 00:02:16,048
the trade-off was actually pretty simple.

47
00:02:16,048 --> 00:02:19,135
Stick too closely to what's safe and you risk starvation

48
00:02:19,135 --> 00:02:20,595
when the grove runs dry.

49
00:02:20,595 --> 00:02:22,263
Stray too far into the unknown

50
00:02:22,263 --> 00:02:25,433
and you might get poisoned or eaten by lions.

51
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:26,642
Luckily for you and me,

52
00:02:26,642 --> 00:02:30,438
the trade offs we face today are far less about survival.

53
00:02:30,438 --> 00:02:33,065
But our choices are still governed by this instinct

54
00:02:33,065 --> 00:02:36,068
to balance caution with curiosity.

55
00:02:36,068 --> 00:02:40,573
If all you ever did in life was play it safe and exploit,

56
00:02:40,573 --> 00:02:42,408
you'd never get disappointed,

57
00:02:42,408 --> 00:02:45,745
but you'd also never get a chance to advance and grow.

58
00:02:45,745 --> 00:02:49,248
If all you did instead was discover and explore,

59
00:02:49,248 --> 00:02:51,542
you'd collect endless experiences,

60
00:02:51,542 --> 00:02:54,921
but you'd also never get a chance to capitalize on your learnings.

61
00:02:54,921 --> 00:02:58,841
So it's really this balance between exploitation and exploration

62
00:02:58,841 --> 00:03:00,593
that fuels our growth,

63
00:03:00,593 --> 00:03:04,639
not just as a species, but also as individuals.

64
00:03:04,639 --> 00:03:08,476
But that's exactly where AI throws a wrench into the works,

65
00:03:08,476 --> 00:03:12,230
because AI hates risk.

66
00:03:12,230 --> 00:03:14,857
And that's not because of its algorithmic DNA.

67
00:03:14,857 --> 00:03:18,110
It's because the systems we rely on to navigate our world,

68
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:20,780
